### What is Union-Find?

Union-Find, also called Disjoint Set Union (DSU), maintains a collection of disjoint sets and supports two operations: find returns a representative root identifying which set an element belongs to, and union merges the sets of two elements. Two elements are in the same set exactly when they share the same root.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class UnionFind {
    private int[] parent;
    private int[] rank;

    public UnionFind(int n) {
        parent = new int[n];
        rank = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }

    public int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]); // path compression
        return parent[x];
    }

    public void union(int x, int y) {
        int rx = find(x), ry = find(y);
        if (rx == ry) return;
        if (rank[rx] < rank[ry]) parent[rx] = ry;
        else if (rank[rx] > rank[ry]) parent[ry] = rx;
        else { parent[ry] = rx; rank[rx]++; }
    }

    public boolean connected(int x, int y) {
        return find(x) == find(y);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UnionFind uf = new UnionFind(5);
        uf.union(0, 1);
        uf.union(2, 3);
        System.out.println("0 and 1 connected: " + uf.connected(0, 1)); // true
        System.out.println("0 and 2 connected: " + uf.connected(0, 2)); // false
        uf.union(1, 3);
        System.out.println("0 and 2 connected: " + uf.connected(0, 2)); // true
    }
}
```

```text
0 and 1 connected: true
0 and 2 connected: false
0 and 2 connected: true
```

### How does the parent array work?

The structure is backed by a `parent[]` array where `parent[i]` is the element that i points to. Initially, every element is its own parent, meaning every element is a singleton set. The representative of a set is its root: the element where `parent[x] == x`.

To find the root, you follow parent links until you reach an element that points to itself. Without optimisations, this chain can be long. Path compression and union by rank keep it short.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ParentArrayDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] parent = {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}; // initial: each is its own root
        System.out.println("Initial parent: ");
        for (int i = 0; i < parent.length; i++)
            System.out.println("  parent[" + i + "] = " + parent[i]);

        // union(1, 0): point 1's root to 0's root
        parent[1] = 0;
        // union(2, 0): point 2's root to 0's root
        parent[2] = 0;
        System.out.println("After unions:");
        for (int i = 0; i < parent.length; i++)
            System.out.println("  parent[" + i + "] = " + parent[i]);
    }
}
```

```text
Initial parent: 
  parent[0] = 0
  parent[1] = 1
  parent[2] = 2
  parent[3] = 3
  parent[4] = 4
After unions:
  parent[0] = 0
  parent[1] = 0
  parent[2] = 0
  parent[3] = 3
  parent[4] = 4
```

### How does path compression work?

Path compression rewrites the chain during find so every node on the path points directly to the root. The clean recursive form is: `if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]); return parent[x];`. This sets each node's parent to the root as the recursion unwinds, flattening the tree for all future lookups.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class PathCompression {
    private static int[] parent = {0, 0, 1, 2, 3}; // chain: 4->3->2->1->0

    public static int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Before: " + Arrays.toString(parent));
        System.out.println("find(4) = " + find(4));
        System.out.println("After:  " + Arrays.toString(parent));
        // All nodes now point directly to root 0
    }
}
```

```text
Before: [0, 0, 1, 2, 3]
find(4) = 0
After:  [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]
```

### How does union by rank work?

Union by rank keeps trees shallow by always attaching the tree with the smaller rank under the one with larger rank. Rank is an upper bound on tree height. Only when two trees have equal rank does the rank increase by one.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class UnionByRank {
    private int[] parent, rank;

    public UnionByRank(int n) {
        parent = new int[n];
        rank = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }

    public int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }

    public void union(int x, int y) {
        int rx = find(x), ry = find(y);
        if (rx == ry) return;
        if (rank[rx] < rank[ry]) parent[rx] = ry;
        else if (rank[rx] > rank[ry]) parent[ry] = rx;
        else { parent[ry] = rx; rank[rx]++; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UnionByRank uf = new UnionByRank(6);
        uf.union(0, 1); // rank[0] becomes 1
        uf.union(2, 3); // rank[2] becomes 1
        uf.union(0, 2); // rank[0] becomes 2 (tie)
        uf.union(4, 5);
        uf.union(0, 4); // rank[0] stays 2 (4's tree is shorter)
        System.out.println("All in same set: " + (uf.find(0) == uf.find(5)));
    }
}
```

```text
All in same set: true
```

### What is the time complexity?

With both path compression and union by rank, the amortised cost per operation is the inverse Ackermann function, written as alpha(n). This function grows so slowly that for any practical input (up to 10^80 elements), alpha(n) is at most 4. This means each find and union is effectively constant time.

Either optimisation alone is weaker. Path compression alone gives O(log n) amortised. Union by rank alone gives O(log n) worst case. Together they achieve the inverse Ackermann bound.

### When is Union-Find used?

Union-Find is the backbone of several classic algorithms and applications:

- **Kruskal's Minimum Spanning Tree** : process edges in weight order, union endpoints, skip if already connected.
- **Cycle detection** : while building a graph, if union finds both endpoints already share a root, the edge creates a cycle.
- **Connected components** : union adjacent elements and count distinct roots.
- **Dynamic connectivity** : answer whether two elements are connected after a sequence of union operations.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CycleDetection {
    private int[] parent, rank;

    public CycleDetection(int n) {
        parent = new int[n];
        rank = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }

    public int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }

    public boolean union(int x, int y) {
        int rx = find(x), ry = find(y);
        if (rx == ry) return false; // cycle detected
        if (rank[rx] < rank[ry]) parent[rx] = ry;
        else if (rank[rx] > rank[ry]) parent[ry] = rx;
        else { parent[ry] = rx; rank[rx]++; }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CycleDetection cd = new CycleDetection(4);
        System.out.println("Add 0-1: " + cd.union(0, 1)); // true
        System.out.println("Add 1-2: " + cd.union(1, 2)); // true
        System.out.println("Add 2-0: " + cd.union(2, 0)); // false: cycle!
    }
}
```

```text
Add 0-1: true
Add 1-2: true
Add 2-0: false
```

### What are the limitations?

Union-Find is not easily decremental. Once two sets are merged, you cannot efficiently split them apart. This means it suits problems where sets only ever grow, such as accumulating fraud links. If you need to remove connections, other data structures like link-cut trees are needed.

### What is the banking use case?

At National Bank of India, Pankaj the architect uses Union-Find to cluster accounts into fraud rings. When the team discovers a link between two accounts, such as a shared device or phone number, Rajesh calls union to merge their groups. When Priya queries whether two accounts belong to the same ring, the system calls find on both and compares the roots. Because the structure stays flat after path compression, these queries are near-instant even as rings grow into the thousands.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FraudRingCluster {
    private int[] parent, rank;

    public FraudRingCluster(int numAccounts) {
        parent = new int[numAccounts];
        rank = new int[numAccounts];
        for (int i = 0; i < numAccounts; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }

    public int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }

    public void linkAccounts(int a, int b) {
        int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
        if (ra == rb) return;
        if (rank[ra] < rank[rb]) parent[ra] = rb;
        else if (rank[ra] > rank[rb]) parent[rb] = ra;
        else { parent[rb] = ra; rank[ra]++; }
    }

    public boolean sameRing(int a, int b) {
        return find(a) == find(b);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FraudRingCluster cluster = new FraudRingCluster(100);
        cluster.linkAccounts(10, 25); // shared device
        cluster.linkAccounts(25, 42); // shared phone
        System.out.println("10 and 42 in same ring: " + cluster.sameRing(10, 42)); // true
        System.out.println("10 and 50 in same ring: " + cluster.sameRing(10, 50)); // false
    }
}
```

```text
10 and 42 in same ring: true
10 and 50 in same ring: false
```
